  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nThis morning's water vapor analysis shows Gulf moisture streaming \nnortherly across the western half of the CWA, while RAP surface \nanalysis reveals a front bisecting the UP from Iron Mountain toward \nGwinn. An elongated surface low, aligning with the front, is \nsandwiched between a mid-level low occupying the northern Plains and \nsouthern Canadian Prairies and broad Atlantic ridging off of \nFlorida. Radar-estimated MRMS totals place a broad swath of rainfall \nupwards of 0.30\" across the western 2/3 of the UP, with some spots \nreaching upwards of 1\". Temperatures sit in the low 50s across the \neast half, and mid 40s across the west. Even with generally light \nflow, a wind shift is visible as clearly in progress, with locations \nin the east still seeing southerly flow and spots in the west and \nKeweenaw seeing northerly or northwesterly flow.\n\nInto this morning, the surface low shifts northeast as steering flow \ntranslates slightly east, causing rainfall to shift toward the \neastern UP by the afternoon. Throughout the day, a mid-level trough \ndrops down from north of Hudson Bay behind these features, resulting \nin falling temperatures throughout the day as cooler air sweeps west \nto east. Rain continues through much of Sunday, with greater than \n60% chance of seeing 1.25\" of rain for the vast majority of the west \nhalf of the UP by 7PM (EST), and lower probabilities tapering off \ntoward the east. As CAA takes hold with northerlies, rain switches \nto snow overnight tomorrow, first in far west and at higher \nelevations during the late evening hours, then becoming widespread \nsnow showers by Monday morning's commute. \n\nWith initial 850mb temps to roughly -8C and a warm lake near \n8C, a mixed marine layer and lake effect clouds will keep \novernight temperatures on Sunday in the 20s and 30s. With low \nSLRs (less than 10:1), still thinking snow will struggle to \naccumulate during the first half of Monday, though multiple \nmodels are showing brief convergence zones in the western UP. \nShould favorable thermodynamics and convergence align, even with\nlower SLRs, ensemble hourly QPF guidance supports instances of \nheavier snowfall leading to periods of lowered visibilities. \nWith colder 850mb temps to -14C arriving during the second half \nof Monday and slightly cooler antecedent ground temperatures, \nSLRs increase and accumulations may be more likely to stick. \nWhile high-resolution guidance continues to indicate decently \nhourly QPF rates (0.1-0.2\"/hr) Monday afternoon, flow appears \nmore uniform with less consensus on convergence bands. Even as \nSLRs increase through the day on Monday with cooler \ntemperatures, totals top out at 5\" in isolated higher terrain \nareas, and 1-4\" elsewhere. \n\nAs ridging and dry air builds quickly in and winds shift westerly, \nlake effect snow ceases by Tuesday morning. Temperatures return\nto seasonable by Monday and remain so through Wednesday, which \nmeans highs in the 30s and lows in the teens (20s near \nshorelines). Some guidance is hinting at a low making its way \nto the Upper Midwest sometime late-week, but details are not \nclear at this time. For now, further precipitation chances \nremain confined to the Friday after Thanksgiving Day.",
